[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one bug, one fix queued. Read by what each report
says, not by its subject line, it holds three problems. One fix will not close it.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix leaves the other two open. Their reports will keep arriving under the
same subjects and keep merging into #4471.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. A day-first tenant's
  7 April becomes 4 July. This is the bug #4471 names.
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Day-first tenants
  type what the hint says, and the form reads it day first. Same symptom, different code:
  the parser fix does not touch it.
- **`shift`**: the date lands one day off, not month-for-day. "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday.
  Client in Toronto got Monday." A month flip cannot do that. The desk already read these as
  a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) and still filed them under `parser` by tenant.

## How it hid

- Reports merge on subject alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
  All three problems produce the same subjects, so all of them landed in one ticket.
- Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. It disagrees with the tenant's cause on
  dozens of reports, so counting by tag gets both numbers wrong.
- The ticket export pages overlap. Count each report id once.
- The incident page shows the split, then footnotes the one-day moves under `parser`:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## What to do

- Track each problem on its own ticket (linked in the table). Close #4471 only when all three are fixed.
- Re-read decision 0001: it was last reviewed in June 2024 and merges reports that are not the same bug.
  A new record should replace it; this page does not edit it.

